Title: Blog about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# When ‘Similar’ Must Stay Similar: The Federal Circuit Demands Consistency Across DuPont Factors

## Why this case matters

In Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp., the Federal Circuit reasserts a simple—but too-often overlooked—principle in trademark opposition practice: once the Board defines the relevant universe of similar goods or services in its analysis of one DuPont factor, it cannot contract that universe without explaining why a different scope is justified for another factor. The court’s decision to affirm the Board’s finding that Apex’s banking services and CC Serve’s credit-card services are highly similar, while vacating and remanding the Board’s narrow treatment of third-party “Aspire” uses under DuPont factor six (and, as consequential, its treatment of factor one) drives home the point that consistency in defining the competitive landscape matters to the ultimate likelihood-of-confusion determination.

Why practitioners should care: many opposition and cancellation cases turn on how broadly the Board defines “similar goods/services.” That definition colors not only the second DuPont factor (similarity of goods/services) but also the sixth (number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods) and the first (appearance, sound, connotation, commercial impression). Apex underscores that you can’t have it both ways—find a broad overlap for confusion reasons and then selectively shrink the comparison pool to evade evidence of third-party use or consumer familiarity.

## The court’s reasoning in brief

The dispute began with CC Serve’s long-standing registration for ASPIRE (credit card services) and Apex’s intent-to-use ASPIRE BANK applications for “banking and financing services.” The TTAB found the services highly similar—indeed, “legally identical, in part”—and gave that substantial weight under DuPont factor two. Apex appealed two particular strands of the Board’s analysis: (1) that the services are highly similar, and (2) that CC Serve’s ASPIRE mark was entitled to a normal scope of protection because third-party uses of “Aspire” were not sufficiently numerous to show weakness.

The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board on factor two (substantial evidence supports the finding of high similarity), but identified a logical error in the Board’s analysis of factor six. Having already concluded that the services align closely, the Board then limited its survey of third-party “Aspire” uses to marks specifically tied to “credit card services,” excluding dozens of other third-party uses of “Aspire” in the broader banking and financial-services space. The court held this narrowing was inconsistent with the Board’s own earlier finding: once the goods/services universe is defined broadly (or found to be highly similar), third-party evidence must be evaluated against that same scope. Because the sixth factor bears directly on the mark’s commercial strength—and commercial strength informs the perception and commercial impression of the mark under factor one—the court vacated both factors one and six and remanded for reconsideration.

Put another way: you cannot find significant overlap between services for purposes of confusion and then decline to consider third-party use that falls within that overlap. The Board’s selective narrowing skewed its assessment of how “weak” or crowded the field is for the ASPIRE-formative marks, and that error required reconsideration.

## Analytical perspective: consistency is a structural necessity in DuPont analysis

A central virtue of the DuPont framework is that it forces the factfinder to make and connect a series of factual findings that support a legal conclusion about likelihood of confusion. That method only works if those factual findings use coherent and consistent definitions. Apex reminds us that defining the relevant consumer market and the scope of competing goods or services is not an ad hoc exercise where the Board can expand or contract the relevant universe depending on whether a particular factor favors one party.

This ruling proceeds from a modest and defensible premise: similarity is similarity. If you determine two services are similar enough that consumers could confuse source, you cannot then treat third-party uses for the same broadly defined services as irrelevant for assessing mark strength. Doing so invites a distortive analysis: a mark might appear strong simply because the Board ignored competing uses that are plainly relevant to consumers who encounter those similar services.

The court’s approach also aligns with prior Federal Circuit precedent recognizing that third-party use need not be for identical goods—only similar ones—to be probative. Olde Tyme Foods and Juice Generation were cited for that proposition, and Apex applies that logic practically. The Board’s mistake was not a novel misreading of precedent; it was an inconsistent application of its own factual findings.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects

Apex will likely have ripple effects in several ways:

1. More rigorous linkage between factors. Boards and courts will need to be careful to carry forward the definitions and market scopes adopted at factor two when assessing factors that logically depend on that scope—particularly factor six (third-party use) and factor one (commercial impression). Expect more remands where the Board’s internal definitions are incoherent.

2. Increased importance of third-party evidence across the broader goods/services class. Parties seeking to show a mark’s relative weakness will have stronger footing to introduce third-party registrations, uses, and marketplace evidence that fall within a broadly defined field of financial services even if the owner’s registration recites a narrower subset (e.g., “credit card services”). Likewise, trademark owners who obtain favorable findings on similarity must be prepared to rebut third-party evidence across that same breadth.

3. Careful evidentiary framing in prosecutions and oppositions. Examining attorneys, opposers, and applicants will need to anticipate that an early definitional choice will have downstream consequences. The Trademark Office and TTAB may be prompted to state more explicitly how they are defining relevant goods and services at different stages of analysis to avoid reversible inconsistency.

4. Possible tactical shifts in identification drafting. Applicants will continue to draft narrow identifications to avoid conflict, but the Apex decision signals that a narrow recitation may not insulate a mark from third-party evidence that covers related services. Conversely, registrants hoping to assert broad protection should expect challengers to marshal third-party uses across the larger field.

## Practice pointers

For practitioners representing applicants, registrants, or challengers, Apex suggests some practical moves:

- Define and document your market early. When litigating or opposing, make an explicit record of how you define the relevant goods/services and the relevant consumer groups. If you argue for a broader overlap (to support confusion), recognize you will have to address any third-party uses within that breadth.

- Be consistent in advocacy. If you argue for high similarity to prove confusion, don’t then argue that the field is too narrow to admit third-party evidence. Conversely, if you argue the services are distinct, make that argument up front and explain why third-party uses across the broader industry are irrelevant.

- Collect broad third-party evidence preemptively. If you represent an owner of a senior mark, collect and analyze third-party uses not only in the exact recited services but in related services—and be ready to argue why those uses are (or are not) relevant. If you represent an applicant/opposee, build a wide net of third-party applications, registrations, and marketplace evidence showing proliferation of similar formative marks.

- Tie commercial strength to the defined marketplace. Because commercial strength is contextual, explicitly connect third-party use evidence to the relevant consumer’s likely experience—what do consumers of “banking and financing services” encounter? How often do they see “Aspire” marks in the broader field?

- Anticipate remand issues. If you win on one DuPont factor that depends on a definitional choice (e.g., services are highly similar), expect the Board to reexamine other factors consistent with that choice. Prepare for that domino effect.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is not a flashy doctrinal pivot. It is, instead, a correction of an internally inconsistent DuPont analysis that had the practical effect of insulating a senior mark from third-party evidence where that evidence plainly fell within a scope the Board had already found relevant. The Federal Circuit’s message is straightforward: once you choose your market lens, you must use it consistently across the relevant DuPont inquiries. For practitioners—whether prosecuting, defending, or opposing marks—the decision is a reminder that definitional discipline matters, and that the interdependence of DuPont factors can turn a single definitional choice into the linchpin of the entire likelihood-of-confusion analysis.



## Counting ‘Aspires’: Third-Party Use Evidence After Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin

Why this case matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve — vacating portions of a Trademark Trial and Appeal Board decision and remanding for further consideration — is a useful reminder that the DuPont likelihood-of-confusion analysis must be coherent across factors. The court didn’t upset the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are highly similar (credit card services versus banking and financing services), but it rejected the Board’s narrowing of the relevant universe of third‑party “Aspire”-formative marks when assessing the sixth DuPont factor (the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods). That holding brings into focus two recurring questions in Lanham Act practice: how many third‑party uses are enough to demonstrate marketplace crowding (and thus a comparatively weak mark), and how should tribunals authenticate and contextualize that evidence — particularly in the financial services space where services overlap and brands proliferate.

What the court said (and why it matters doctrinally)

Two strands of precedent frame the Federal Circuit’s ruling. On the one hand, cases like Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin made clear that a field saturated with similar marks can justify a narrower scope of protection for a challenger’s mark; courts can point to “a considerable number” of third‑party uses (Juice Generation cited 26), or “voluminous evidence” (Jack Wolfskin discussed fourteen notable examples among many) to show weakness by dilution of distinctiveness in the marketplace. On the other hand, older decisions hold that the sixth DuPont factor does not demand identical goods or services — only similar ones. The Board’s error was procedural and conceptual: having concluded (in its second‑factor analysis) that Apex’s banking and financing services and CC Serve’s credit card services are highly similar (even partially legally identical), it then considered only third‑party marks used on credit card services and excluded marks used on other banking/finance services. The Federal Circuit sensibly rejected that narrowing. If the services are effectively in the same commercial orbit, one cannot arbitrarily constrict the field of third‑party evidence to a subcategory of services when assessing whether the mark is crowded.

This is more than a quibble about labels. The better (and required) practice is internal consistency: findings about the relationship between goods/services should inform — not be divorced from — the DuPont factors that follow. Otherwise a Board may overstate the strength of an earlier mark by selectively parsing the third‑party landscape.

Quantity versus quality: what it takes to show crowding

Apex’s submission included nine third‑party marks for credit card services and other submissions showing a further 42 Aspire‑formative marks for broader banking and finance services. The Board treated the nine as insufficiently numerous to demonstrate weakness and dismissed the rest as irrelevant because they were not limited to “credit card services.” The Federal Circuit’s remand invites the Board to reassess the third‑party evidence with the correct scope — including related banking/finance uses — but it leaves open how many and what kinds of third‑party uses will suffice.

From the post-Juice and Jack Wolfskin landscape, a few practical principles emerge:

- Pure count matters, but so does salience. Courts have been swayed by both large numbers of third‑party uses and by a set of examples that together show a pattern of widespread use. There is no fixed numeric threshold. Juice Generation’s 26 and Jack Wolfskin’s references to “voluminous” evidence are illustrative, not prescriptive. A handful of third‑party registrations or uses can be persuasive if they are prominent, national, long‑standing, and illustrative of a crowded marketplace; conversely, many obscure uses limited to a narrow geographic or niche market may matter less.

- Services must be meaningfully related. Third‑party uses tied to services that customers would reasonably associate with the same source as the opposed services carry more weight. That is precisely the Federal Circuit’s point here: because banking/finance and credit card services overlap in commerce (and in the Board’s own second‑factor ruling), marks used across those categories are relevant to whether the Aspire mark has been diluted by competitive use.

- Evidence of consumer sophistication and marketplace context can cut both ways. In some markets, numerous similar marks may show that consumers are accustomed to distinguishing brands (supporting a finding of non‑confusion); in others, proliferation may simply mean commoditization, raising the bar for distinctiveness. The financial sector — with many subbrands (cards, bank divisions, program names) and intermediated relationships (banks partnering with processors or program managers) — often presents the ambiguous middle ground where context and provenance matter a great deal.

Authentication and contextualization of third‑party evidence

A recurring practical pinprick in Board and court decisions is the quality of third‑party evidence. Registrations, screenshots, advertising copies, press releases, and domain records all have different evidentiary weight. The Apex remand underscores how a Board must not only collect a sufficiently broad set of marks when warranted by the similarity finding, but also must authenticate and contextualize them.

- Authentication: Third‑party use evidence should be supported by reliable source material — archival web captures, contemporaneous marketing material, printouts with URL/meta data and capture dates, or sworn declarations from witnesses with knowledge. The PTO and TTAB accept a variety of exhibit types, but the trier of fact must be able to evaluate whether an item reflects genuine commercial use and when it was used.

- Contextualization: Not all “uses” are created equal. The Board on remand should assess geographic scope (local vs. national), duration, intensity of use (heavy national advertising versus passive domain holding), and channels (same channels of trade as the opposed services?). For financial services, where white‑label, co‑brand, and programmatic offerings are common, it also matters whether the third party used the mark as the primary source indicator or as a program/product name under a larger corporate house mark. The difference affects how consumers might perceive source.

- Consumer perception evidence: If the factual record permits, showing whether consumers actually perceive Aspire‑formative marks as signaling a common source — via surveys, testimony, or market studies — can be dispositive, though such evidence is costly and often unnecessary where the aggregate documentary record is compelling.

Implications for financial services marks

The financial services sector is a crowded and brand‑rich environment. Banks, processors, card networks, fintech platforms, and white‑label partners often create program names that migrate across institutions. That marketplace reality both complicates and informs DuPont inquiries.

Practitioners should take away three practical points. First, when opposing or defending mark applications in finance, be prepared to cast a wide net of third‑party evidence if the services overlap: program or product names across banking and card services are probative. Second, anticipate battles about proof — don't rely on bare lists of registrations or isolated screenshots; substantiate the evidence and explain its commercial relevance. Third, tailor the narrative: a challenger seeking to show crowding should emphasize national/sustained uses and consumer exposure; an applicant seeking to criticize third‑party evidence should be ready to show narrow use, limited reach, or different channels of trade.

Downstream doctrinal effects

The Federal Circuit’s remand is narrowly tailored — it does not announce a new rule of law — but it reinforces an important structural principle: DuPont factors must be analyzed consistently. A board’s conclusion about the relatedness of services should not be used to narrow the scope of evidence considered under other factors unless there is a reasoned basis to do so. The decision therefore nudges tribunals toward more holistic DuPont narratives and will likely result in more careful, cross‑factor reasoning in future TTAB opinions.

Practically, we should expect litigants to press harder for inclusion of seemingly adjacent categories of services when marshaling third‑party evidence, and to litigate the evidentiary foundation for such inclusions. Expect also more emphasis on contextual evidence in the financial space: the presence of many “Aspire” uses alone may not be dispositive, but their number, prominence, and commercial reach — properly authenticated and contextualized — could tip the sixth DuPont scale toward crowding and a narrower scope of protection.

Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is a useful course correction: when the Board concludes services are closely related, it cannot then cherry‑pick a narrow slice of third‑party uses to defeat a showing of marketplace crowding. The decision preserves the Board’s finding of service similarity, but insists on coherent treatment of third‑party evidence across the DuPont analysis. For practitioners in the crowded financial sector, the ruling reinforces the importance of both casting an appropriately broad evidentiary net and of carefully documenting and contextualizing every third‑party use you rely upon.



# Credit Cards, Banks, and Program Managers: Defining ‘Credit Card Services’ in a Post–ID Manual World

## Why this case matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a timely reminder that the way trademarks are described matters — especially in the shifting landscape of banking, fintech, and program-manager arrangements. The opinion affirms that “credit card services” can overlap substantially with more general “banking and financing” services, while also policing how the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) analyzes the competitive field when the Trademark ID Manual no longer supplies a tidy definition. For fintech program managers, issuing banks, and traditional banks launching online sub-brands, this case affects both how to draft identifications of services and how to evaluate risk of opposition from prior marks.

At bottom, the Federal Circuit did three things: (1) affirmed the Board’s factual finding that Apex’s intended “banking and financing services” are highly similar to CC Serve’s longstanding “credit card services” registration (the second DuPont factor); (2) vacated the Board’s treatment of third-party “Aspire” marks (the sixth DuPont factor) because the Board narrowed the relevant universe of third-party uses too much after it had already found high similarity between the parties’ services; and (3) vacated and remanded the Board’s first-factor (marks’ similarity/commercial impression) analysis for reconsideration in light of a corrected sixth-factor inquiry. The practical upshot: when services are found to overlap, the Board must consider third-party uses across that entire scope — and that, in turn, may alter the perceived strength of a registered mark and the overall likelihood-of-confusion calculus.

## How the Board tried to fill the ID Manual gap — and why the Circuit pushed back

A procedural wrinkle drove much of the dispute: “credit card services” had once been an entry in the Trademark ID Manual, but that entry was deleted after CC Serve’s registration issued. The Board therefore reconstructed the meaning of “credit card services” from evidence: it read the term to cover issuing cards, processing transactions, account management, monitoring and alerts, authorization services, and counseling regarding card debt. With that reconstructed definition in hand, the Board concluded the parties’ services were highly similar — in part legally identical — because banking and finance services encompass the issuance of credit and related account services. The Federal Circuit affirmed that conclusion as supported by substantial evidence.

So far, this is a straightforward application of the DuPont test: when goods/services overlap in nature, channels, or consumer perception, the “similarity of the services” factor can cut heavily toward a finding of likelihood of confusion. The wrinkle — and the reversible error identified by the court — came next. Having found high similarity, the Board limited its duPont-six analysis (third-party uses) to only those third-party marks explicitly tied to “credit card services,” excluding third-party “Aspire” marks used across other banking and finance services. The Board reasoned that marks for other services were “essentially irrelevant.” The Federal Circuit rejected that approach: once the Board itself has found the services highly similar (or partially legally identical), it must treat the universe of potentially relevant third-party uses consistently. The sixth factor does not demand identical goods; it demands consideration of marks used on similar goods. Narrowing the scope at that stage undercuts the purpose of the sixth factor — to assess commercial strength or crowding across the relevant marketplace.

Because the number and nature of third‑party “Aspire” uses could materially affect the commercial strength attributed to CC Serve’s mark, the court vacated and remanded the sixth‑factor finding and, prudently, remanded the first‑factor analysis as well (marks’ overall commercial impression), since that inquiry is influenced by the mark’s perceived strength.

## What this means for fintech program managers and bank–fintech partnerships

Modern credit-card programs are often split arrangements: a fintech may run the user-facing product and program management, while a bank issues the card and appears as the regulated party on the account. Apex Bank highlights that, for likelihood-of-confusion analysis, courts and the Board will look beyond corporate labels to the services as described and perceived. If an applicant’s identification of services encompasses issuing credit or managing accounts — even if the applicant itself is not the regulated issuing bank today — such services can be sufficiently similar to classical “credit card services” to trigger conflict with an earlier mark in that space.

For fintech program managers, the decision has two practical implications. First, offering program‑management functions that substantially overlap with aspects of card issuance, authorization, account management, or billing can place you in the same “service universe” as traditional credit-card providers for trademark purposes. That increases the risk that a prior word mark used in connection with credit-card services will block your registration. Second, the outcome reinforces that submitting specimens and identifications that accurately and narrowly describe your role is critical. Where possible, identify your services in a way that differentiates program‑management or software‑as‑a‑service functions (e.g., “program management services for third‑party banks in connection with credit card programs” or “software for managing credit card rewards programs”) from the regulated act of issuing credit or maintaining customer accounts.

Issuing banks and fintech partners should also be mindful in contract drafting and branding. If a fintech’s mark appears on the card and in customer communications, courts may view the services as emanating from a common source (even when the bank is the legal issuer). Joint branding, co‑marks, and prominent placement of the fintech’s trademark on the card or portal can increase the prospect of confusion with an earlier mark.

## What traditional banks and sub‑brands should take away

Traditional banks launching sub‑brands or internet banking offerings (like Apex’s planned ASPIRE BANK mark) should understand that a generic-seeming term (e.g., Aspire) used in a bank sub‑brand may collide with existing credit‑card marks, even those owned by program managers or fintechs that operate through issuing-bank partnerships. The Federal Circuit’s affirmation that “banking and financing services” can be highly similar to “credit card services” means the two categories are not siloed for comparison.

If your sub‑brand will involve account issuance, card issuance, or services that customers reasonably associate with credit, search and clearance should include not just bank marks but also marks used by program managers and fintechs in the payments ecosystem. And if a prior mark is identified, be prepared for the Board or a court to consider third‑party uses across a wide slice of the financial-services marketplace in assessing whether the prior mark is conceptually weak.

## Drafting guidance for modern identifications — practical tips

1. Be precise about the actor and function. If you are a program manager or SaaS provider, make that explicit: “program management services for credit card programs provided by issuing banks” or “software for processing credit card transactions for third‑party banks.” Explicit role descriptions help draw a line between your services and the act of issuing credit.

2. Limit the recitation of services to what you actually offer. Avoid broad omnibus terms like “banking and financing services” unless you truly mean them. Broad terms invite apples‑to‑oranges comparisons (and, as here, apples may be treated like oranges when evidence shows overlap).

3. Include channels and classes of customers when appropriate. If your services are limited (e.g., “provided online” or “to small‑business merchants”), including that restriction can narrow the scope of similarity in practical perception.

4. Use specimens that reflect the services named. The TTAB and courts rely on specimens to understand how the mark is actually used. If your specimens show a clear distinction (e.g., a platform for banks rather than consumer‑facing card accounts), that can support a narrower perception of goods/services.

5. Consider coexistence and consent agreements. If you operate in a crowded field, negotiating coexistence or consent agreements with prior users can be an efficient way to clear registration risk, but beware of terms that concede scope more broadly than intended.

6. Perform clearance searches across the ecosystem. Include program managers, issuing banks, processors, and fintechs in search queries. Prior marks may live under different corporate names and still be relevant.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects

Apex Bank confirms two doctrinal points that should guide practitioners. First, the ID Manual’s evolving contents do not insulate parties from ordinary evidentiary work: when the Manual no longer clarifies a term, the TTAB must reconstruct meaning from record evidence — and that reconstruction is entitled to substantial‑evidence review. Second, and more consequentially for practitioners, once the Board has found that services are highly similar, it cannot then cabin the sixth DuPont inquiry to a narrower slice of third‑party uses. Doing so undermines the very purpose of the sixth factor: measuring the commercial field and the relative weakness or strength of a mark across similar services. The Federal Circuit’s remand requires the Board to take a more holistic view when services overlap, which could lead to a finding that a seemingly strong mark is, in fact, conceptually or commercially weaker because of multiple third‑party uses across the relevant marketplace.

For case law and practice, expect more robust examination by the TTAB of third‑party uses across adjacent financial services once a similarity finding is made. That will benefit applicants who can show that a mark-formative term is crowded across banking and payments uses, but it will increase the evidentiary burden on owners of prior marks to establish commercial strength within the full universe of similar services.

## Bottom line

Apex Bank v. CC Serve underscores that in the post‑ID Manual world, careful drafting and honest, precise evidence about the nature of services matter. Fintechs, program managers, and banks can’t rely on corporate form or labels to immunize a mark from comparison to legacy credit‑card registrations. Likewise, trademark opponents and registrants should be ready to litigate the scope of the relevant marketplace — and the Board must consider third‑party uses across that full scope when services are found to overlap. For brand owners in the payments ecosystem, the safest course is clarity: specify who does what, show how customers perceive the service, and search broadly across the modern financial‑services landscape.



## Strength First, Similarity Second? Why the Federal Circuit Vacated the First DuPont Factor

Why this case matters: The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a reminder that the DuPont likelihood-of-confusion analysis is an integrated exercise. In a seemingly narrow interlocutory move—vacating the Board’s findings on the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks) and the sixth (third‑party use)—the court underscored that a mark’s commercial strength or weakness can materially alter the “appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression” analysis. The opinion is important not because it upends DuPont, but because it clarifies the sequencing and interdependence of DuPont factors: when the Board’s own findings (here, that the parties’ services are highly similar) bear directly on what third‑party evidence is “similar enough” to dilute a mark’s strength, the Board cannot compartmentalize its review. This decision should push trademark practitioners and the TTAB toward a more holistic treatment of commercial strength before finalizing conclusions about mark similarity.

What the court did: The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that Apex’s proposed ASPIRE BANK mark and CC Serve’s earlier ASPIRE registration applied to highly similar services (the second DuPont factor). But the court vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth DuPont factor—number and nature of third‑party marks—because the Board, after concluding the services were highly similar, proceeded to consider only third‑party uses that were limited to “credit card services,” excluding many third‑party uses of “Aspire” in other banking and financing contexts. The Federal Circuit held that this narrowing was legally erroneous: similarity under factor six need not be limited to identical goods or services, and where the Board already found high similarity between services, it should adopt a matching scope when evaluating third‑party uses bearing on commercial strength. Because commercial strength (or weakness) informs the overall commercial impression central to the first DuPont factor, the court remanded the first factor as well for reconsideration in light of any revised assessment of third‑party use. In short: first reassess the field, then reassess how consumers experience the marks.

The court’s core reasoning and its limits: Two points drive the court’s approach. First, the DuPont factors are fact‑intensive and interrelated; a factual finding under one factor (here, service similarity) constrains the scope of relevant evidence under another (six). Second, evidence that a mark is “crowded”—numerous third‑party uses of similar marks on related services—can lessen a mark’s commercial strength and thus narrow its scope of protection. The Board’s mistake was methodological: it accepted that the services overlapped substantially for purposes of the second factor but then tightened its lens when considering third‑party evidence. The Federal Circuit did not announce a new rule that the first factor must always follow a sixth‑factor analysis; rather, it required that the Board apply consistent definitions of similarity across factors so that the Board’s own findings are not self‑contradictory.

A practical roadmap for integrating commercial‑strength evidence into the first‑factor analysis: The opinion invites a more structured approach on remand—one that practitioners should bear in mind.

- Define the relevant universe of services and consumers at the outset. If the Board finds (as it did here) that services are “highly similar” or partially legally identical, it should adopt that scope when determining what third‑party registrations and uses are “on similar goods or services” under factor six. Narrowly limiting the universe at factor six after finding broad similarity at factor two creates analytical tension.

- Assess third‑party evidence with parity. When third‑party uses of the same (or similar) term appear across the broader field of related financial services, the Board should consider whether such use is widespread enough to suggest that consumers have been conditioned to distinguish among ASPIRE‑formative marks. That assessment should focus on both the number of such uses and their nature—market visibility, geographic scope, and whether the third parties operate in channels likely to be encountered by the same consumers.

- Translate third‑party use into commercial strength findings. If factor six shows a crowded field, the Board should explicitly find that the prior mark is conceptually or commercially weak—or at least entitled to a narrower scope of protection. Those findings should explain how consumer expectations and marketplace realities reduce the likelihood that consumers would be confused by similar marks.

- Revisit “appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression” in light of strength. The first DuPont factor asks how the marks will be perceived; that perception is not developed in a vacuum. If consumers routinely encounter many ASPIRE variants in related financial contexts, the Board should factor that conditioning into the first‑factor analysis—asking, for example, whether ASPIRE has distinctive connotations in this field or functions more as a descriptive or suggestive element. Where a mark’s strength is diminished, the Board should be explicit about how that diminishment reduces the weight of similarities in appearance or sound.

- Explain how strength shifts the likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus overall. The Board’s ultimate likelihood finding should tie the threads together: show how service similarity, the number and nature of third‑party uses, and the resulting commercial impression of the marks converge (or diverge) to make confusion more or less likely.

Doctrinal implications and downstream effects: Apex Bank reiterates long‑standing principles—third‑party use can erode a mark’s scope and the DuPont factors are interdependent—but it also signals that the Federal Circuit will police methodological consistency. Boards that treat DuPont factors as siloed checklist items risk reversal if earlier factual findings are inconsistent with narrower evidentiary choices later in the analysis. Practically, litigants should press for a clear sequence at the TTAB: define the relevant marketplace and consumers; consider third‑party uses across that market; make explicit strength findings; and then use those findings when analyzing mark similarity. For trademark owners, this decision is a warning: an older registration’s priority date helps, but commercial ubiquity of similar marks across related services can blunt protection. For applicants, the case underscores the utility of marshaling broad third‑party evidence to show dilution of distinctiveness—and pressing the Board to fold that evidence into its first‑factor analysis.

Concluding perspective: The Federal Circuit’s remand in Apex Bank is not a technicality; it is an invitation to the TTAB to treat commercial strength as more than a passing consideration. The proper sequence is not necessarily “strength then similarity” as matter of fixed order, but it is insistence on coherence: factual findings about the marketplace and service overlap must inform the ambit of third‑party evidence and, through that, the commercial impression consumers will take from the marks. On remand, the Board has a relatively straightforward task—expand its lens to include third‑party uses across related banking and financing services, decide whether ASPIRE is crowded in that broader field, and then reconsider how, if at all, that crowding changes what consumers will perceive when they see ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK. That integrated approach will produce a more defensible likelihood‑of‑confusion analysis and clearer guidance for practitioners.



# Standard of Review in TTAB Appeals: Substantial Evidence vs. Legal Error in Apex v. CC Serve

## Why Apex v. CC Serve matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. (Fed. Cir. Sept. 25, 2025) is a useful road‑map for practitioners litigating trademark oppositions at the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) and on appeal. It is a paradigmatic mixed‑disposition: the court affirms the Board’s factual finding (that the parties’ services are highly similar) under the substantial‑evidence standard, but vacates and remands parts of the Board’s DuPont analysis as legal error—most notably the Board’s overly narrow treatment of third‑party “Aspire” marks under the sixth DuPont factor and, as a consequence, its first‑factor analysis of overall commercial impression.

The case sharpens the boundary between deferential appellate review of fact findings and plenary review of legal rulings about the correct scope or standard to be applied. That boundary is where many TTAB appeals live. Apex confirms two practical points: (1) when the Board’s conclusions rest on record facts supported by substantial evidence, the Federal Circuit will not disturb them; but (2) when the Board applies an incorrect legal rule—here, by narrowing the universe of third‑party marks considered under factor six inconsistent with its own finding on factor two—the court will vacate and remand for reconsideration.

## What the Federal Circuit did and why

Two points drove the outcome.

First, the court affirmed the Board’s finding under the second DuPont factor that credit‑card services (CC Serve’s identified services) and “banking and financing services” (Apex’s identification) are highly similar and, in part, legally identical. The Board did the standard work: define the identified services, consult dictionary definitions and entries in the Trademark ID Manual, and consider third‑party registrations showing overlap. On appeal the Federal Circuit applied the familiar test—legal questions de novo, fact findings for substantial evidence—and concluded the Board’s factual findings were amply supported. That meant the second DuPont factor could stand as decided.

Second, and crucially, the court identified a legal mistake in the Board’s sixth‑factor analysis. The sixth factor looks to the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods and can show commercial weakness (and thus a narrower scope of protection) if the field is crowded. The Board had, however, confined its examination to third‑party marks identified only for “credit card services,” excluding many third‑party uses of “Aspire” across the broader banking/finance landscape. That narrower universe contradicted the Board’s own earlier finding that the parties’ services are highly similar (indeed, partially legally identical). The Federal Circuit held that, because DuPont factor six examines uses on similar—not identical—goods, the Board must use a similarly broad scope when counting and characterizing third‑party uses. Limiting consideration to only exact matches on “credit card services” was a legal error requiring vacatur and remand. Because commercial strength and third‑party crowding can materially affect the marks’ overall commercial impression, the court also remanded the first DuPont factor for reconsideration.

In short: affirm the fact finding on similarity (substantial evidence), vacate the sixth‑factor analysis as legal error, and remand both the sixth and the first factors for reconsideration consistent with the Board’s finding of high similarity.

## Analytical perspective: doctrine and coherence

Apex highlights an important doctrinal coherence principle: the Board’s treatment of a given DuPont factor should not be logically inconsistent with its treatment of another where the same underlying definitional choice governs both. Here the Board settled on a broad conceptual overlap between “credit card services” and “banking and financing services” for factor two, yet then narrowed the lens for factor six. The Federal Circuit’s remedy—insist the scope be consistent—reflects sound appellate housekeeping. It prevents the Board from having it both ways: relying on a broad relatedness rationale to find likely confusion while excluding relevant evidence that could undermine the strength of the cited mark.

The decision also reaffirms familiar but consequential points about TTAB appeals. The Board is entitled to deference on pure fact findings supported by substantial evidence, including assessments of services’ similarity when based on dictionary entries, registrations, and other record evidence. But when the Board employs an incorrect legal test—here, a too‑narrow conception of “similar” in the sixth factor—that is a legal error subject to correction. That division is predictable but practically important: many appeals turn on whether an argument can be reframed as a challenge to the sufficiency of evidence (hard to win on appeal) or as a dispute over the correct legal standard (a stronger appellate posture).

Finally, the opinion underscores the substantive interplay of DuPont factors: evidence that a field is crowded with similar marks can narrow the senior mark’s protection, which in turn can change the weight given to visual/sound/connotation similarities. The Board must perform that balancing transparently and consistently.

## Practical implications and downstream effects

For TTAB practitioners and appellants, Apex suggests several tactical takeaways.

First, keep the record broad when arguing relatedness. If you assert that services are “related” across a product line, preserve and develop evidence of third‑party use across that same breadth. Courts will expect consistency: a Board cannot find services closely related for one factor and then ignore relevant third‑party uses for another.

Second, when defending a registration, build a robust record on commercial strength or weakness. Third‑party registrations and evidence of marketplace use are crucial. The Federal Circuit relied on the small number of “Aspire” uses limited to credit‑card services to find that the Board had insufficient evidence to deem the mark weak—but it vacated because the Board artificially constricted the universe of comparable uses. Parties should therefore introduce third‑party evidence across the entire spectrum of services that the parties themselves claim are related. Include registrations, real‑world examples (web pages, trade publications), and, where possible, sales/use data to show whether a term is crowded or distinctive in the broader category.

Third, preserve legal arguments about scope and standard. Apex succeeded on appeal because it could point to a legal inconsistency—the Board’s adoption of incompatible scopes across DuPont factors. Appellate courts are receptive to arguments that ask them to enforce consistent legal standards rather than second‑guess factual inferences. If you want the Federal Circuit to revisit a Board ruling, frame at least one of your main contentions as a pure legal question (e.g., whether the Board applied an incorrect formulation of “similarity” for factor six, or whether the Board’s definition of the relevant public was legally erroneous).

Fourth, think strategically about framing evidence problems as legal errors. Not every misstep is a persuasive legal issue on appeal; arguing that the Board “erred in weighing” evidence without pointing to a legal rule is a weaker position. Better to show that the Board applied a wrong legal test or an internally inconsistent standard (as the Federal Circuit found here). The line between “insufficient evidence” and “incorrect application of law” is sometimes narrow—specifically articulate where the Board’s approach clashes with precedent (e.g., DuPont, Palm Bay, Juice Generation, Jack Wolfskin, Olde Tyme Foods).

Finally, remember that remand is a real remedy. The Federal Circuit did not substitute its own balancing for the Board’s; it sent the case back to ensure the Board applies the correct scope. That outcome often gives the prevailing party at the TTAB a second chance if the record can be augmented or the Board’s reassessment favors them on reconsidered factors.

## How to preserve issues and build the record in TTAB proceedings

A few concrete practice points follow from Apex:

- When alleging relatedness of services, explicitly identify the boundaries of the “relevant public” and the relatedness rationale you expect the Board to apply. If you argue relatedness broadly (banking/finance), tie any third‑party‑use evidence to that same broad category.

- Proactively collect third‑party evidence across the full range of allied services: registrations, screenshots, pricing pages, product/service descriptions, and any industry commentary that shows the terms of art (for example, whether “Aspire” is used for both issuing credit cards and other banking products). Authentication and clear topic tagging in the record make this evidence easier to weigh on appeal.

- Offer, and if necessary litigate for, discovery aimed at showing commercial strength or weakness: marketplaces, number of users, co‑existence agreements, licensing, and consumer surveys (where feasible). Surveys can be expensive, but when the question is crowdedness and consumer recognition, they can move the needle.

- Preserve legal objections to scope at the TTAB hearing and in post‑hearing briefing. Ask the Board to state its definitional choices explicitly so those choices are reviewable on appeal.

- If you expect to appeal, preserve transcripts and carefully identify contested factual findings and legal questions. The Federal Circuit will affirm factual findings supported by substantial evidence; reversible error is likelier where the Board’s rule or scope is inconsistent with precedent or internally contradictory.

## Conclusion

Apex v. CC Serve is a tidy lesson in appellate posture for trademark litigants: substantial‑evidence review is a high bar for upsetting factual findings, but appellate correction is readily available when the Board applies an incorrect legal standard or an inconsistent scope across related DuPont factors. Practitioners should aim both to build a deep evidentiary record and to frame a clean legal question for appeal. That dual approach—robust facts, preserved legal issues—is the best way to ensure a TTAB case survives the gauntlet of mixed standard review.



# Who Is the “Relevant Public” for Financial Services Marks?

## Why this case matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. matters because it revisits a recurring tension in trademark law: how to define the “relevant public” when assessing the weight of third‑party uses under the sixth DuPont factor. That tension is particularly acute in the financial services space, where services (banking, lending, payment processing, card issuing, account servicing) often overlap functionally even when they are offered through different business models and partners. The Board’s original decision narrowed the universe of third‑party marks to consumers of “credit card services” alone; the Federal Circuit rejected that narrowing and remanded, holding that the Board must treat the scope of third‑party use consistently with its earlier factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar. The ruling sharpens an important lesson: once a tribunal has found that services are similar (or partially legally identical) under DuPont factor two, it cannot cabin the sixth‑factor inquiry to a narrower consumer subset without explaining why a different scope is appropriate.

Why practitioners should care: factor six evidence is often decisive in gauging the commercial strength of a mark and therefore the breadth of protection it enjoys. Courts’ willingness (or not) to construe the “relevant public” narrowly can change the outcome of a likelihood‑of‑confusion inquiry, particularly in crowded or descriptive fields. Apex clarifies that the Board must remain faithful to its own service‑similarity findings when defining which third‑party uses matter.

## What the Federal Circuit did

The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s conclusion that Apex’s “ASPIRE BANK” marks and CC Serve’s prior ASPIRE registration concern highly similar services (DuPont factor two). But it found legal error in the Board’s treatment of the sixth factor. The Board, having concluded the services overlapped considerably, nevertheless limited the sixth‑factor inquiry to third‑party Aspire marks used specifically for “credit card services,” excluding Aspire uses in other banking and financing contexts. The court held that was inconsistent: the sixth factor asks about the number and nature of similar marks “on similar goods,” and similar—not identical—goods are sufficient for the Board to consider. Because the Board had already accepted that the parties’ services were highly similar (indeed partly legally identical), restricting the sixth‑factor universe to a narrower subgroup of consumers was unsound. The Federal Circuit therefore vacated the sixth‑factor finding and also vacated the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks) for reconsideration, since a revised assessment of third‑party use could alter the marks’ commercial impression and relative strength.

## The doctrinal point: consistency across DuPont factors

At bottom, Apex reinforces a straightforward doctrinal principle: the scope of the “relevant public” for purposes of weighing third‑party uses must be tethered to the Board’s (or court’s) findings about the relatedness or similarity of the parties’ goods or services. DuPont factor two is about product/service similarity; factor six is about the marketplace density of similar marks that might weaken a proprietary mark’s reach. If you find two services sufficiently similar to warrant comparison, you cannot then adopt a stricter, narrower definition of the relevant consumer group for factor six without explaining why the narrower scope is analytically justified.

Two commonly recurring errors this opinion pushes back on:

1) Cherry‑picking the consumer subset most likely to help the result. The Board in Apex effectively narrowed the consumer definition only for factor six—despite finding broader service overlap under factor two. The Federal Circuit barred that move.

2) Treating the “buyer of the product” as the only relevant referent for evaluating third‑party weakness when the product categories themselves were found broader. As the court said, similarity for factor six does not require identity; so third‑party marks in closely related banking/finance channels are relevant even if they are not labeled “credit card services.”

## How this fits with prior case law

Apex sits comfortably with prior Federal Circuit instructions that the DuPont factors must be applied coherently. The court has long held that the sixth factor looks to similar—not identical—goods or services (Olde Tyme Foods; Juice Generation), and that findings under one DuPont factor inform the others (see Bose, M2 Software). Apex simply enforces that cross‑factor logic: if your factor‑two finding sweeps broadly, your factor‑six canvas must too.

The decision also aligns with cases that treat third‑party evidence as probative of a mark’s commercial weakness where such evidence demonstrates widespread, similar use in the relevant market (Palm Bay, Jack Wolfskin, Juice Generation). But it underscores that the question of what counts as the “relevant market” is not free‑floating; it depends on the Board’s factual conclusions about similarity. Apex does not lower the bar for showing weakness—indeed, the Federal Circuit left untouched the Board’s ultimate conclusion that CC Serve had not shown the ASPIRE mark to be so widely used as to be weak—but it requires the Board to consider the correct universe of marks when making that judgment.

## Practical implications for litigants and the Board

For litigants in trademark oppositions and cancellations—especially in financial services and other crowded industries—the Federal Circuit’s ruling has immediate tactical and evidentiary consequences.

For plaintiffs (prior users/registrants seeking to block a later mark):
- Expect challengers to argue that third‑party uses should be limited to a narrow consumer group when that narrows the evidence of crowding. Apex warns that such a narrowing will be resisted if the defendant has already shown service overlap across a broader field.
- If you want the Board to use a narrower “relevant public” in assessing third‑party use, build the record at factor two to support a distinction: demonstrate distinct channels of trade, different end users, and materially different consumer sophistication or decisionmaking contexts between the specific subcategory and the broader field. Don’t rely on post hoc narrowing.

For applicants (those seeking registration of a mark):
- Assemble a broad record of third‑party uses not only in nominally identical categories but in all closely related financial services channels if you aim to show the mark is weak. Apex highlights that evidence of Aspire‑formative marks across banking, lending, and payments—not only card issuing—can be relevant when services overlap.
- When opposing a likelihood‑of‑confusion defense grounded in a prior mark, emphasize differences in channels of trade, consumer profiles, and marketing that justify a more focused relevant public. But be prepared for the Board to require more than labels—show the practical marketplace distinctions.

For the Board and lower tribunals:
- Be explicit about the consumer universe you adopt for factor six and anchor that choice to the findings made under factor two. If you decline to carry over factor‑two scope, explain why a different frame is warranted (for example, specific differences in channels of trade or consumer sophistication for the sixth‑factor inquiry).
- When considering third‑party registrations and uses, state the reasons for including or excluding related categories; a terse conclusion that some uses are “essentially irrelevant” without tying that decision to the factual record risks reversal.

## Downstream effects and unresolved questions

Apex will be cited in future TTAB and district court opinions as a caution against inconsistent DuPont treatment. It will matter most in industries where branding overlaps across adjacent services—financial services, healthcare, telecommunications, software-as-a-service—because these sectors routinely involve partnering and layered service delivery models (banks partnering with processors, fintechs white‑labeling cards, insurers offering loans, etc.).

Two issues remain fertile for dispute. First, how granular must a Board’s justification be when it chooses a narrower relevant public for factor six? The Federal Circuit did not prescribe a formula; it demanded coherence. So, tribunals will need to develop a more textured practice of explaining why consumers of a subcategory are the right referent even when service similarity has been found broadly. Second, the decision may increase the burden on litigants to develop a robust marketplace record early—demonstrating channels of trade, how products are marketed, and who buys them—to support a preferred scope of the “relevant public.”

## Conclusion

Apex makes a simple but consequential point: you cannot have your DuPont cake and eat it too. If the Board finds services to be highly similar (or even partially legally identical), it must consider third‑party uses across the same scope when evaluating the number and nature of similar marks under factor six. That holding should improve doctrinal coherence in crowded fields and push parties to develop records that align their arguments about consumer scope across factors. In practical terms, the opinion rewards disciplined, market‑focused factual development and penalizes snap legal shortcuts that narrow the relevant public in a way that conflicts with findings already made about product or service relatedness.



# The Perils of Aspirational Branding: ‘ASPIRE’ in a Crowded Financial Services Field

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful reminder that aspirational, dictionary-based brand terms—words like ASPIRE—do not enjoy broad, monopolistic protection in crowded commercial fields. The Federal Circuit’s decision, issued September 25, 2025, affirms that similarity of services can carry an applicant a long way in a Section 2(d) fight, but it also vaccinates against a too-narrow view of third‑party evidence of use. On remand the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board (TTAB) must take a fresh look at the number and nature of third‑party “ASPIRE” marks across financial services, and that reconsideration could materially narrow the survival prospects of CC Serve’s two‑decade‑old registration. For practitioners, the opinion underscores the strategic importance of third‑party evidence in both clearance and opposition proceedings, and it illustrates how the DuPont analysis remains a multi-factor balancing test in which an initial finding on one factor (here, services similarity) should control the scope of inquiry on others (here, the universe of comparable marks).

## The court’s reasoning in brief

The Federal Circuit affirmed the TTAB’s conclusion on the second DuPont factor: Apex’s proposed “ASPIRE BANK” marks for banking and financing services and CC Serve’s registered ASPIRE mark for credit card services are highly similar and, in part, legally identical. That factual finding is grounded in dictionary definitions and in third‑party registrations linking credit card services and banking/financing services; it is therefore reviewed for substantial evidence and sustained.

But the court reversed (vacated) the TTAB’s handling of the sixth DuPont factor, which asks whether there are many similar marks in use on similar goods. The TTAB confined its sixth‑factor inquiry to third‑party marks used specifically for “credit card services,” excluding other marks that used ASPIRE in the broader banking and financial services space. The Federal Circuit held that this was inconsistent: having already found the parties’ services to be highly similar, the Board should not then employ a narrower view of “similar goods/services” when considering evidence that could diminish the registered mark’s strength. Because the sixth factor was misapplied, the court also vacated the TTAB’s first‑factor analysis (similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation, and commercial impression), reasoning that the commercial strength or weakness revealed by third‑party use can materially affect the overall commercial impression. The case is affirmed-in-part (second factor), vacated-in-part (first and sixth factors), and remanded for reconsideration.

## What the opinion means for doctrinal doctrine

Three doctrinal points bear emphasis.

First, the opinion reaffirms that DuPont is an interlocking inquiry. Findings under one DuPont factor—especially a central one like similarity of services—should shape the scope of other factors. The Board erred by treating “credit card services” as the relevant universe for third‑party mark comparisons after already concluding that the parties’ services overlap broadly with banking and finance. The Federal Circuit’s correction preserves the coherence of the multi-factor analysis: you do not get to pick a narrow definition for some factors and a broader one for others when doing otherwise would skew the balancing.

Second, the decision underscores the continued vitality of third‑party use evidence to show relative weakness or a narrow scope of protection for inherently distinctive marks. Palm Bay, Juice Generation, and Jack Wolfskin remain touchstones: a crowded field of similar marks can teach consumers to distinguish among marks and thus lessen the chance of confusion. The TTAB’s prior conclusion that only nine uses existed—insufficient to show weakness—was vacated not because the court believed those nine uses necessarily prove weakness, but because the Board improperly ignored an arguably larger body of evidence that could reveal a more crowded marketplace. On remand, if the Board considers the broader set of third‑party ASPIRE usages in banking and finance, it may well find the mark entitled to narrower protection.

Third, the case illustrates the spectrum of trademark strength. Even inherently distinctive words can be relatively weak within an industry when competitors adopt similar aspirational language. ASPIRE is a paradigmatic example: the term has positive, generic connotations in the financial context (aspiration, goal‑setting), which makes it attractive to multiple actors and, when many adopt it, harder for any single registrant to block others from related uses.

## Practical consequences: clearance, coexistence, and enforcement

For trademark owners and brand advisers, Apex Bank v. CC Serve has immediate, practical implications.

Clearance. Before launching an aspirational mark in the financial sector, thorough clearance must go beyond the federal registration database. Counsel should compile third‑party use evidence across the full relevant slice of services (not an artificially narrow subset) to assess whether the field is crowded. The Federal Circuit’s remand suggests the TTAB must consider uses of ASPIRE across banking, lending, payment, and allied financial services when gauging mark strength. A clearance opinion that ignores that broader commercial reality risks overestimating enforceability.

Coexistence and branding strategy. Where third‑party use is substantial, pursuing coexistence—through careful brand architecture, disclaimers, or geographic/market segmentation—may be more realistic than all‑out exclusionary litigation. Aspirational marks like ASPIRE invite natural clustering (e.g., ASPIRE Savings, ASPIRE Rewards), and parties may be able to coexist if they distinguish channels of trade, customer base, or visual branding. The court’s correction encourages a pragmatic approach: when the field is crowded, courts and the TTAB will be less likely to grant a single registrant sweeping rights.

Enforcement. For owners of older registrations, Apex is a caution: a registration alone does not guarantee broad protection if the marketplace demonstrates dilution by multiple similar marks. Enforcement actions should factor in third‑party use evidence early; aggressive challenges to newcomers may fail if the registrant’s mark is shown to sit amid many similar uses in the same commercial sphere. Conversely, challengers (and applicants) should be proactive about assembling third‑party use evidence to argue for narrow scope or weakness.

Litigation tactics. The opinion signals that parties should tailor their DuPont proofs to reflect the interconnectedness of the factors. If you win on services similarity under factor two, the Board must align its comparison universe accordingly under factor six. Litigants should therefore prepare third‑party evidence that covers that full universe at the outset. Conversely, registrants should anticipate and rebut such broad evidence by focusing on actual consumer perception, long‑standing exclusive use, survey evidence, or other proofs of acquired distinctiveness and commercial strength.

## How the remand could recalibrate ASPIRE’s scope

On remand, the TTAB will now be expected to consider the broader set of third‑party ASPIRE and ASPIRE‑formative marks across banking, financing, and credit services. If that review uncovers the 42 additional marks Apex pointed to—uses across bank branding, lending programs, and payments—the TTAB could conclude that ASPIRE is commercially weak within the financial services field and therefore entitled to a narrower scope of protection. That outcome would make it harder for CC Serve to block Apex’s ASPIRE BANK application, particularly given the Board’s already-affirmed finding that the services overlap. Narrow protection could limit CC Serve’s ability to prevent other financial institutions from using ASPIRE‑formative trademarks, especially where consumer sophistication, channels of trade, or branding differences reduce actual confusion.

Alternatively, the Board could find the additional third‑party uses insufficiently probative—for instance, if they are geographically limited, use different stylizations, or target distinct customer classes. In that event CC Serve’s registration might retain its “normal” protection and the rejection of Apex’s mark could be sustained. The Federal Circuit’s remand does not dictate a particular outcome; it only enforces a coherent analytical framework that lets relevant market evidence move the scale.

## Bigger picture: balancing protection and competition in common-word branding

Apex v. CC Serve sits at the intersection of two competing trademark values. On one hand, trademark law protects consumers from likely confusion and protects brand owners who have invested in identifying their goods and services. On the other hand, the law resists permitting a single actor to monopolize everyday language that other market participants have legitimate reasons to use—whether for descriptive utility (e.g., “aspire” as an encouragement to save or improve finances) or persuasive branding.

The Federal Circuit’s decision enforces that balance by requiring the TTAB to account for the marketplace reality when assessing mark strength. Aspirational words are particularly susceptible to dilution by widespread adoption; a robust evaluation of third‑party use helps ensure that trademark rights reflect commercial reality rather than formalistic siloing of services.

## Bottom line for practitioners

A few practical takeaways follow from Apex Bank v. CC Serve:

- When advising clients on aspirational marks in crowded industries, build clearance records that synthetically and empirically demonstrate third‑party use across all relevant services and channels.
- If a registrant has an older registration but the industry has since filled with similar marks, counsel should prepare to present evidence of exclusive use, consumer recognition, or surveys to rebut claims of weakness.
- In oppositions and litigation, insist that the Board apply a consistent scope across DuPont factors; winning (or losing) on services similarity should not allow parties to cherry‑pick a different similarity definition elsewhere.
- Consider coexistence frameworks early. Where third‑party use is ubiquitous, negotiated coexistence and careful brand differentiation may be more valuable than an expensive, uncertain legal fight.

Aspirational branding is appealing, but in finance—where short, evocative words carry massive persuasive value—monopoly over a commonplace term is hard to achieve if competitors also find it useful. The Federal Circuit’s remand in Apex preserves the TTAB’s role in weighing market reality, and on remand the fate of ASPIRE will turn on whether the Board concludes the financial services field is indeed crowded enough to narrow CC Serve’s protection.



# Third-Party Registrations vs. Marketplace Use: Two Edges of the Same DuPont Sword

## Why this case matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful reminder that the DuPont likelihood-of-confusion analysis is not a set of silos. Third-party evidence can be deployed in multiple, sometimes inconsistent-seeming ways: to show that different services are “of a type that may emanate from a single source” (supporting similarity under DuPont factor two), and to show that a term or mark is commercially weak because many others use it (DuPont factor six). The Court affirmed the Board’s conclusion that Apex’s proposed ASPIRE BANK marks and CC Serve’s registered ASPIRE mark cover highly similar services. But it vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth and, accordingly, the first DuPont factors because the Board artificially narrowed the universe of third‑party marks it considered when assessing commercial weakness. The panel’s ruling reinforces a core evidentiary principle: consistency in defining the relevant scope of goods and consumers matters across DuPont factors.

Practitioners should take note: the Federal Circuit did not change the law about what counts as “similar” services or what third‑party evidence can do; it simply insisted that the Board apply the same reasonable scope to both queries. That has immediate practical consequences for how parties should assemble and present third‑party registration and marketplace‑use evidence.

## What the court said, in plain terms

Two parts of the Board’s analysis drove the appeal. First, the Board concluded (and the Federal Circuit affirmed) that CC Serve’s “credit card services” and Apex’s “banking and financing services” are highly similar and, in part, legally identical. That finding was grounded in definitions, industry practice, and third‑party registrations showing that entities commonly offer both kinds of services under the same mark.

Second, when the Board turned to DuPont factor six — the “number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods” — it significantly narrowed the universe of third‑party marks it would consider. Although Apex had offered dozens of third‑party uses of “Aspire” across the broader financial services space, the Board limited its inquiry to marks expressly tied to “credit card services” and excluded marks used for related banking and financing services. On that truncated record, the Board found only nine relevant third‑party uses and concluded that CC Serve’s mark was not shown to be weak. The Federal Circuit held that limitation was legal error. Because the Board had already (and correctly) found the services highly similar under factor two, it was inconsistent and impermissible to require a stricter notion of similarity for purposes of factor six. The court vacated the Board’s factor six finding and — because commercial strength feeds into the marks’ overall commercial impression — remanded factor one as well.

## Why the court’s approach is correct (and useful)

Two principles animate the opinion.

First, a DuPont analysis rests on interlocking factual findings. If the Board establishes that the relevant consumers and services encompass a broad range of banking and credit‑related offerings, it cannot then adopt a narrower evidentiary lens when assessing how many other parties use similar marks in that same space. The same universe of goods/services and consumers should inform both similarity and weakness analyses, absent a persuasive reason to differentiate.

Second, the opinion underscores how third‑party registrations and marketplace use play different but complementary roles. Registrations (and Trademark ID Manual entries) are useful to show how services are classified and how the examining public or USPTO historically treats particular descriptions — helpful when deciding whether services are related. Marketplace use evidence (real‑world branding, actual product names, consumer‑facing materials) is the classic way to show dilution of commercial strength. The Board’s error was not relying on registrations per se; it was cherry‑picking which third‑party evidence applied to which factor.

This is doctrinally modest but practically important: the Federal Circuit is policing consistency, not elevating one kind of evidence above another. That fit is what makes the decision a good reminder for how to build a record.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects

The opinion will likely influence both how the Board evaluates DuPont proofs and how practitioners litigate oppositions and cancellations:

- Consistency across factors. Boards and courts will be more attentive to whether the same factual scope—who the consumers are, what services are encompassed—applies across related DuPont factors. Parties cannot win by arguing a broad scope for similarity and a narrow scope for third‑party weakness (or vice versa) without justification.

- Broader use of marketplace evidence. Because the Federal Circuit required the Board to consider third‑party marks across the same range of services it had found similar under factor two, applicants and opposers should expect more comprehensive consideration of marketplace uses. That increases the value of well‑documented real‑world evidence (screenshots, trade literature, declarations).

- Registration evidence still matters, but only in context. Third‑party registrations and the Trademark ID Manual remain important for establishing how services are routinely classified and related, but registrations alone may be insufficient to prove commercial weakness. Conversely, strong marketplace use across the broader class can tend to narrow a mark’s scope of protection.

- Tactical consequences in prosecution and oppositions. Applicants should resist overly broad descriptions that invite comparisons. Opposers should be prepared to counter registrations with convincing marketplace evidence that a mark enjoys commercial strength despite other registrations. Conversely, applicants or third parties seeking to prove weakness should collect evidence across the whole field the Board treats as relevant.

- Remands and re-litigation. The opinion suggests more remands where the Board’s scope choices are inconsistent. Expect additional development of the record on re‑examination or remand, not immediate finality.

## A practical checklist for developing third‑party evidence (for applicants and opposers)

Below is a practical approach to building the two complementary sides of the DuPont third‑party story. The goal is to avoid the kind of scope inconsistency that doomed the Board’s factor six analysis in this case.

1. Define the scope up front and be consistent
   - From the outset, define the relevant goods/services and the relevant consumer class and channels of trade, and use that definition consistently across DuPont factors. If you need to argue a narrower scope for a persuasive reason, articulate and support that reason with evidence.

2. Use registrations to show classification and common sourcing
   - Collect third‑party registrations that pair the contested services (e.g., “credit card services”) with broader categories (e.g., “banking and financing services”) to support relatedness under factor two.
   - Include serial numbers, registration dates, and specimens where available. If an ID Manual entry was deleted (as happened here), explain its historical meaning and any resulting ambiguity.

3. Collect marketplace use for commercial strength/weakness
   - Gather real‑world evidence of third‑party use of the formative term across the same service universe: screenshots, advertisements, product pages, service agreements, press releases, and app listings.
   - Time‑stamp and archive materials (Wayback Machine, affidavits) to show the duration and geographic extent of use. Demonstrate prominence (e.g., national rollouts, partnerships).

4. Quantify and contextualize
   - Don’t just produce lists. Explain how many third‑party uses are national vs. local; whether usage is incidental or core to the business; overlaps in channels of trade and consumers.
   - If you claim a crowded field, show whether competitors are sizable players or obscure niche offerings—both matter for whether consumers are “educated” to distinguish marks.

5. Present declarations from industry witnesses
   - Use declarations from industry professionals, distributors, or knowledgeable consumers to explain market practices: whether banks routinely offer credit cards under the same brand used for everyday banking, whether consumers expect unified branding, and how consumers encounter these services.

6. Tie evidence to the relevant DuPont factors explicitly
   - Explain how each piece of evidence bears on factor two (relatedness), factor six (third‑party presence and weakness), and factor one (commercial impression), rather than dumping material into the record and leaving the Board to make inferences.

7. Anticipate counterarguments about quality vs. quantity
   - If an opponent relies on a handful of registrations, be prepared to show active marketplace use or, conversely, distinguish permutations as not genuinely competitive (different channels, different consumers, limited duration).

8. Track priority and timing
   - Be mindful of priority dates (registration/first use) and, when relevant, the timing of third‑party uses relative to the parties’ marks. Use of a term after a registrant’s priority date can still bear on weakness, but the Board will examine probative value carefully.

9. Consider supplemental evidence on remand
   - If a court remands on factor six (as here), be ready to supplement the record. A remand is an opportunity to present broader evidence consistent with the Board’s findings on relatedness.

## Bottom line

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is not a doctrinal revolution. It is, instead, a corrective: the Federal Circuit insisted that the Board not wear two different lenses when examining relatedness and third‑party weakness. For litigators and prosecution counsel, the lesson is practical and immediate—be comprehensive and consistent. Third‑party registrations and marketplace use are different edges of the DuPont sword; know when to use each, but don’t let them point in different directions.